WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.960
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.200
Today we are going to create a virtual world&nbsp;
with breathtakingly detailed pieces of geometry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.120
These are some results from a new technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:22.240
and boy, are they amazing. I wonder how expensive&nbsp;
this will get. We’ll see about that in a moment.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:30.080
Now, here , we wish to create a virtual world, and&nbsp;
we want this world to be convincing, therefore we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.760
will need tons of high-resolution geometry, like&nbsp;
the ones you see from our previous episodes.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:44.000
But all this detail in the geometry means tons of&nbsp;
data that has to be stored somewhere. Traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.040
techniques allow us to crank up the detail in the&nbsp;
geometry, but there is a price to be paid for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:58.240
And the price is that we need to throw more memory&nbsp;
and computation at the problem. The more we do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.400
the higher the resolution of the geometry&nbsp;
that we get. However, here comes the problem.&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:12.640
Ouch. Look. At higher-resolution levels, yes,&nbsp;
the price to be paid is getting a little steep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:19.760
Hundreds of megabytes of memory is quite steep&nbsp;
for just one object, but wait, it gets worse!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:27.840
Oh, come on. Gigabytes? That is a little too&nbsp;
much. Imagine a scene with hundreds of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.480
objects laying around, no graphics&nbsp;
card has enough memory to do that.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:40.240
This new technique helps us add small bumps and&nbsp;
ridges to these objects much more efficiently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.640
than previous techniques. This opens up the&nbsp;
possibility for creating breathtakingly detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:53.840
digital objects, where even the tiniest&nbsp;
imperfections on our armor can be seen. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.360
that is wonderful. But let’s compare to how&nbsp;
previous techniques can deal with this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.000
and see if this new one is any better.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:09.360
Here is a traditional technique at its best,&nbsp;
when using 2 gigabytes of memory. That is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:18.640
expensive. And here is the new technique. Hmm!&nbsp;
Look at that - this looks even better. Surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:26.400
this needs even more memory. How many gigabytes&nbsp;
does this need? Or, if not gigabytes, how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.720
hundreds of megabytes? What do you think? Please&nbsp;
let me know in the comments section. I’ll wait.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:40.800
Thank you! I am super excited to see your guesses.&nbsp;
Now, hold on to your papers, because actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:48.240
it’s not gigabytes. Not even hundreds&nbsp;
of megabytes. It is 34 megabytes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.440
That is a pittance for a piece of geometry&nbsp;
of this quality. This is insanity.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.880
But wait, it gets better! We can even&nbsp;
dynamically change the displacements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:07.360
on our models. Here is the workflow - we plug&nbsp;
this geometry into a light simulation program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:14.880
and we can change the geometry, the lighting&nbsp;
material properties, or even clone our object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:21.920
many-many times, and it still runs interactively.&nbsp;
What about the noise in these images?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.440
This is a light simulation technique called&nbsp;
path tracing, and the noise that you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:34.320
here slowly clears up over time as we simulate&nbsp;
the path of many more millions of light rays.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.760
If we let it run for long enough, we end&nbsp;
up with a nearly perfect piece of geometry.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.560
I wonder how the traditional&nbsp;
technique is able to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:50.000
if it is also given the same memory&nbsp;
allowance? Well, let’s see…wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:59.360
there is no contest here. Even for other cases, it&nbsp;
is not uncommon that the new technique can create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:06.960
an equivalent, or better geometry quality&nbsp;
but use 50 times less memory to do that.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.040
So from now on, we might get&nbsp;
more detailed virtual worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.520
for cheaper. Sign me up right&nbsp;
now! What a time to be alive!

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:24.960
And, one more thing. When checking out the&nbsp;
talk for the paper, I saw this. Whoa…20 views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:32.720
20 people have seen this talk. Now, I always&nbsp;
say that views are, of course, not everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.360
but I am worried that if we don’t talk&nbsp;
about it here on Two Minute Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.600
almost no one will talk about it. And these&nbsp;
works are so good, people have to know!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.960
And if you agree, please spread the word on&nbsp;
these papers and show them to people you know!

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

